  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing the Mockups**\n\nI\'m now diving into the user\'s Figma mockups for the \"Campus Lost and Found\" app. I\'m starting by dissecting the \"Dashboard - My Logged Items\" screen, focusing on the header elements first. Understanding this initial screen is key to building the app\'s structure.\n\n\n**Deconstructing the Interface**\n\nI\'ve examined the \"Dashboard - My Logged Items\" screen, now I\'m shifting to the \"Matches\" tab. The layout features rows detailing matches between reported items. I noted the visual distinction between \"Your Item\" and the matched item, along with the percentage indicator, and location detail. The focus is on clarity for matching items.\n\n\n**Refining the Matching Logic**\n\nI\'m now iterating on the matching interactions within the app. My focus is on making the match process dynamic and interactive for the user. I\'ve been considering different states for matches (confirmed, rejected, pending), and action buttons such as \"Message Finder,\" \"Not a Match,\" and \"View Chat\". The goal is an intuitive experience. I want a smooth transition to a chat interface upon a positive match.\n\n\n**Designing the Homepage**\n\nI\'m now sketching the main homepage, focusing on clear calls to action. I want to highlight the core functions: \"I Lost Something\" and \"I Found Something\". I\'m playing with a mockup image on the right, perhaps a visual card or a generic illustration to convey the purpose. Also, I\'m thinking about including some dynamic stats to show the app\'s activity. The goal is to provide a compelling, informative user entry point. I\'m visualizing the data stats now, as well as a \"Tips for Success\" callout.\n\n\n**Visualizing the Chat Interface**\n\nI\'m now deep in the message interface design, specifically focusing on the chat windows. I\'ve designed the list view on the left, complete with search functionality and the key conversation metadata. The chat window itself is taking shape. I\'m focusing on key elements like avatars, conversation titles, and the all-important \"Match Confirmed\" badge (or \"Confirm Match\" button) which will be essential to guide users through the process.\n\n\n**Developing the Interaction**\n\nI\'m now designing the interactive chat interface! A significant feature will be the \"Confirm Match\" button transitioning to \"Match Confirmed\". I\'ve created the chat messages, showing user and sender messages in distinct bubbles. I\'m focusing on the input area for real-time message sending; users can type and submit messages that will immediately appear. I\'ve designed the foundational elements for a seamless user experience.\n\n**Designing the Form**\n\nI\'m now designing the \"Report a Found Item\" form, with the title \"Report a Found Item\" and subtitle: \"Help someone find their lost item by providing as much detail as possible.\" I\'ve specified key fields: Item Name, Category, Detailed Description, Location Found, and Specific Location. I\'m planning placeholders for each form field. I am adding a \"Tip: More details help us match with lost items faster\".\n\n\n**Constructing the Layout**\n\nI\'m now detailing the \"Report a Found Item\" and \"Report a Lost Item\" forms. I\'ve added date and time fields in a two-column layout. I\'ve designed the dashed upload card for photos, and I\'m building out the \"Submit\" and \"Cancel\" buttons.\n\n\n**Defining the Sign-In/Up Flows**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the account creation and login processes. I\'ve designed both the Sign-In and Create Account forms. Key fields like matric number, password, and social login options are in place. The button styling will ensure consistency with the app\'s established visual identity, including the use of an orange-red Submit button to match the theme.\n\n\n**Refining the Sign-Up Process**\n\nI\'ve just added the \"Create Account\" form with fields for full name, matric number, and school email (with the OAU Ife domain specified). Password fields (with confirmation) are included. Social logins (Google, Apple) are integrated with a clear \"Or\" divider. The goal is a clean, comprehensive sign-up experience.\n\n\n**Implementing Local State**\n\nI\'m now focusing on local state management to deliver a feature-rich, instantly usable application. I\'m building it around mock user accounts and standard data interactions to simulate real-world behavior. The goal is to provide a complete, interactive experience without needing credentials or external services. I can now provide the user with full client-side state.\n\n\n**Developing the Frontend**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in building a responsive React frontend, utilizing Tailwind CSS for styling. I\'m focusing on perfectly replicating the designs, including color schemes, component layouts, and precise element placement. I\'ll use local state and `localStorage` for a persistent, interactive experience. I\'ve considered potential use cases for the skill `image-generation`, but since we can style everything to match, it\'s not needed.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 15
      nanos: 897505145
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
